DuckDB in storage/rapid
=======================

The RAPID secondary engine runs its queries in DuckDB (MIT license,
https://duckdb.org).  The sources of DuckDB are not in this tree: they are
downloaded when the plugin is built.

  1. When the build directory is configured, the release to use is decided:

       -DRAPID_DUCKDB_VERSION=latest   (the default) the latest release, asked
                                       from the GitHub API
       -DRAPID_DUCKDB_VERSION=v2.0.0   that release

     "latest" is resolved once for a build directory and kept in the CMake
     cache (RAPID_DUCKDB_TAG), so that configuring again does not change the
     release under a build.  To move to a newer release:

       cmake -URAPID_DUCKDB_TAG .

  2. "make rapid" downloads the source archive of the release
     (https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/archive/refs/tags/<tag>.tar.gz, or
     -DRAPID_DUCKDB_URL=<url or file> for a mirror or a copy), and builds it
     with DuckDB's own CMake files in <build>/storage/rapid/duckdb-<tag>:
     static libraries, position independent, without jemalloc, the shell and
     the tests.

  3. All the static libraries of that build (the core, its third party
     libraries, and the extensions that DuckDB links statically:
     core_functions, parquet, json, icu) are put into one,
     libduckdb_bundle.a (duckdb_bundle.cmake), which is linked into
     ha_rapid.so.

The engine only uses the C API of DuckDB (duckdb.h), which is stable across
releases.  It was built and tested with v2.0.0.  Building needs a network
connection the first time (or RAPID_DUCKDB_URL), about 110 MB of download and
1.3 GB in the build directory.  -DWITHOUT_RAPID_SECONDARY_STORAGE_ENGINE=1
builds the server without the engine.

DuckDB does not publish the single file amalgamation (duckdb.cpp /
duckdb.hpp) any more since 2.0, which is what this engine was built with
before.
